# Why won't the market build a lighthouse?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What makes a good public: non-rival and non-excludable? Why did Coase find that private lighthouses existed after all?"
next: {"title":"Who looks after what everyone owns?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/47-who-looks-after-what-everyone-owns.cast"}

## Ten boats, no light
setting: {"ground":"sky/water/45"}
sources:
    - id: mill1848
      title: Principles of Political Economy, Book V, ch. XI
      authors: John Stuart Mill
      year: 1848
      finding: No one would build lighthouses from personal interest unless indemnified from a compulsory levy made by the State.
    - id: samuelson1964
      title: 'Economics: An Introductory Analysis, 6th ed.'
      authors: Paul A. Samuelson
      year: 1964
      finding: A businessman could not build a lighthouse for profit, since he cannot claim a price from each user.

    path land closed #8f887c points [[985,160],[760,160],[705,230],[730,315],[800,342],[900,340],[985,350]] style.fill #e9dfc7 hidden true
    polygon tower dashed #3d3833 points [[800,342],[862,342],[850,560],[812,560]] hidden true
    polygon lamp dashed #3d3833 points [[806,560],[856,560],[856,610],[806,610]] hidden true
