    icon boat_7 size 80 x 235 y 210 icon_look picture of sailboat hidden true
    icon boat_8 size 80 x 350 y 210 icon_look picture of sailboat hidden true
    icon boat_9 size 80 x 465 y 210 icon_look picture of sailboat hidden true
    icon boat_10 size 80 x 580 y 210 icon_look picture of sailboat hidden true
    group fleet members ["boat_1","boat_2","boat_3","boat_4","boat_5","boat_6","boat_7","boat_8","boat_9","boat_10"] hidden true
    scratch sc work ["10 boats × $300 = $3,000","cost: $2,000 a year"] x 320 y 540 font_size 38 hidden true
    portrait mill x 130 y 510 width 190 of "John Stuart Mill" hidden true
    text millq "Mill, 1848: only a\n“compulsory levy made by the State”" cites ["mill1848"] x 500 y 560 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Ten boats, no light"

Why won't the market build a lighthouse? Take Harbourtown's harbour mouth: a reef, and in a bad win (@draw wreck@)ter, a boat on it.
    draw land reef reef_lab lh

    erase wreck

B: Say ten fishing boats use the harbour, and each skipper would happily pay $300 a year for a light. Illustrative numbers.
    draw fleet parallel true

That's $3,000 a year of value. And say the lig (@draw sc_line_2@)ht costs $2,000 a year to run.
    draw sc_box sc_line_1

B: So the light is worth a thousand dollars more than it costs.
    highlight sc effect box

    quiz "Would a private business build the Harbourtown light?" judge false voice b pool false
        right: Hold on to your answer.
        * Yes: value beats cost
        * No: it can't make boats pay
        * Only if it can charge in the harbour

    erase sc

Economists said no for more than a century. John Stuart M (@draw mill@)ill wrote in 1848 that no one would build lighthouses out (@draw millq@) of personal interest, unless paid from a compulsory levy made by the state.

    erase mill millq

B: Paul Samuelson repeated it in his best-selling textbook: a businessman can't claim a price from each user.
    text samq "Samuelson: “cannot claim\na price from each user”" cites ["samuelson1964"] x 500 y 560 font_size 34

    erase samq

Here's the trap. The skipper o (@highlight boat_3 effect circle@)f boat three thinks: if the others pay, the light shines on me anyway.
    polygon beam #f2c14e points [[810,592],[40,330],[40,120],[810,580]] style.fill #f2c14e style.opacity 0.25
    text think1 "“they'll pay”" x 320 y 385 font_size 32

    erase think1

B: And if they don't, my $300 alone won't build it. Either way, I keep my money.
    text think2 "“keep my $300”" x 320 y 385 font_size 32

    erase think2

Every skipper (@fade fleet to 0.4@) reasons the same way. That's the free-rider problem.
    text fr "free riders" #b5482e x 300 y 520 font_size 44

B: So the hat goes round and comes back with far less than $2,000.
    text coll "collected:\nfar below $2,000" #b5482e x 560 y 580 font_size 34

    erase beam fr

The light is worth more than it costs, and nobody builds it. Not for lack of wanting, but because nobody can be made to pay.
    highlight lh effect pulse

    pause 0.5

## Rival? Excludable?
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    row_label: Excludable?
    col_label: Rival?
    row_values:
      - 'Yes'
      - 'No'
    col_values:
      - 'Yes'
      - 'No'
    cells:
      - [Private, Club]
      - [Common pool, Public]
    highlight:
      - [1, 1]
    label_size: 34
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 175
      w: 920
      h: 470
sources:
    - id: samuelson1954
      title: The Pure Theory of Public Expenditure
      authors: Paul A. Samuelson
      year: 1954
      finding: 'Collective consumption goods: one person''s use leaves no less for others; efficient provision sets the sum of marginal benefits equal to marginal cost.'
    - id: samuelson1964
      title: 'Economics: An Introductory Analysis, 6th ed.'
      authors: Paul A. Samuelson
      year: 1964
      finding: Even if keepers could charge every ship (say by radar), it would cost society zero extra to let one more ship use the light, so a toll would not be optimal.

    icon ic_bread size 50 x 442 y 375 icon_look picture of bread hidden true
    icon ic_cin size 50 x 783 y 375 icon_look picture of cinema hidden true
    icon ic_fish size 50 x 442 y 207 icon_look picture of fish hidden true
    icon lhi size 50 x 783 y 207 icon_look picture of lighthouse hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch sc work [{"tex":"\\text{private good: each buyer, } MB = MC"},{"tex":"\\text{public good: build if } v_1+\\dots+v_{10} \\ge C"},{"tex":"10\\times\\$300=\\$3{,}000 \\ge \\$2{,}000"},{"tex":"\\text{but each alone: } \\$300 < \\$2{,}000"}] x 500 y 430 font_size 38 hidden true

    card title "Rival? Excludable?"

Free riding sank the light. What is it about a light that invites it? Two things.
    draw grid

B: First, rivalry. Mia's loaf is ri (@highlight col_header_0 effect circle@)val: if I eat it, you can't. A beam is non-r (@highlight col_header_1 effect circle@)ival: boat eleven sees it just as well as boat one.
    draw col_title col_header_0 col_header_1

So what's the second?
    draw row_title

B: Exclusion. Mia keeps the loaf behind the counter until you pay. Nobody  (@draw row_header_1@)can dim the beam for one boat that hasn't paid.
    draw row_header_0

Cross the two and you get four kinds of goods. Rival and excludable: private goods, like bread.
    draw cell_0_0 ic_bread

B: Excludable but not rival: club goods, like a cinema. Ri (@draw cell_1_0 ic_fish@)val but not excludable: fish in the open sea.
    draw cell_0_1 ic_cin

And neither rival nor excludable: public goods. The lighthouse, national defence, clean air.
    draw hl_1_1 cell_1_1 lhi

    ask "A toll motorway, outside rush hour: which box? Tap it." choose ["cell_0_0","cell_0_1","cell_1_0","cell_1_1"] answer cell_0_1 voice b pool false
        right: Club: a barrier keeps out drivers who don't pay, and on an empty road one more car slows nobody.
        wrong: Can a barrier keep non-payers out? Does one more car slow anyone? Here, club.

B: Now change one assumption. Samuelson imagined a keeper who could charge every passing ship, say by radar.
    highlight lhi effect circle

Then the light jumps to the club box. Problem solved?
    move lhi by [60,168] duration 1.5 ghost true

B: Samuelson said no. One more ship costs society nothing extra, so any toll turns away boats that would have used the light for free.
    highlight col_header_1 effect circle

So non-rivalry is the deeper problem. Even when you can charge, a price above zero wastes some of the light.
    highlight col_title effect underline

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want the condition in symbols?

@math

    erase mathq

    fade grid cell_0_0 cell_0_1 cell_1_0 cell_1_1 hl_1_1 ic_bread ic_cin ic_fish lhi row_title col_title row_header_0 row_header_1 col_header_0 col_header_1 to 0.12

For a private good, each buyer goes on until her marginal benefit equals marginal cost.
    draw sc_box sc_line_1

B: For a public good you add up everyone's value: build if the sum beats the cost.
    draw sc_line_2

Harbourtown: three thous (@draw sc_line_4@)and beats two thousand, yet each skipper's own three hundred never does.
    draw sc_line_3

    erase sc

@test

    erase mathq

    fade grid cell_0_0 cell_0_1 cell_1_0 cell_1_1 hl_1_1 ic_bread ic_cin ic_fish lhi row_title col_title row_header_0 row_header_1 col_header_0 col_header_1 to 1

## Coase goes to the archives
sources:
    - id: coase1974
      title: The Lighthouse in Economics
      authors: Ronald H. Coase
      year: 1974
      finding: '1610–1675: no lighthouses built by Trinity House, at least 10 by private individuals; of 46 lighthouses in 1820, 34 had been built by private individuals; tolls per light passed, collected by customs at the ports; the 1836 Act vested the lights in Trinity House, which bought out the rest by 1842.'
    - id: vanzandt1993
      title: 'The Lessons of the Lighthouse: "Government" or "Private" Provision of Goods'
      authors: David E. Van Zandt
      year: 1993
      finding: 'The private lighthouses relied on the state at every step: patents, compulsory tolls, collection.'
    - id: bertrand2006
      title: 'The Coasean analysis of lighthouse financing: myths and realities'
      authors: Elodie Bertrand
      year: 2006
      finding: Private lighthouse finance depended on state-enforced charges; voluntary payment did not last.
    - id: trinity
      title: Light Dues (Trinity House)
      authors: Trinity House
      year: 2026
      finding: Lighthouses in the UK and Ireland are funded by light dues on commercial ships calling at UK and Irish ports, paid into the General Lighthouse Fund.

    text claim "“only the state can build it”" #8f887c x 500 y 628 font_size 36 hidden true
    mark claim_x around claim kind strike hidden true
    portrait coase x 860 y 420 width 200 of "Ronald Coase" cameo true hidden true
    text n1610 "1610–1675: Trinity House built 0,\nprivate builders at least 10" cites ["coase1974"] x 500 y 470 font_size 36 hidden true
    text n1820 "34 of 46" #2f6b8f cites ["coase1974"] x 500 y 330 font_size 96 hidden true
    text n1820b "lighthouses in 1820 built privately" #2f6b8f x 500 y 245 font_size 30 hidden true
    box ship "Ship" x 140 y 345 width 220 height 110 radius 14 icon ship icon_side left font_size 36 hidden true
    box port "Port: customs" x 480 y 345 width 320 height 110 radius 14 icon anchor icon_side left font_size 36 hidden true
    box owner "Owner" x 845 y 345 width 230 height 110 radius 14 icon lighthouse icon_side left font_size 36 hidden true
    box crown "Crown" x 665 y 500 width 230 height 110 radius 14 icon crown icon_side left font_size 36 hidden true
    arrow e1 ship -> port hidden true
    label e1l "toll" below attach_to e1 hidden true
    arrow e2 port -> owner hidden true
    label e2l "dues" below attach_to e2 hidden true
    arrow e3 crown -> owner hidden true
    label e3l "patent" right attach_to e3 hidden true
    arrow e4 crown -> port dashed #b5482e hidden true
    text e4l "enforces" #b5482e x 490 y 455 font_size 30 hidden true
    text buy "1836: Trinity House buys them out" #b5482e x 500 y 232 font_size 34 hidden true
    text st1 "free riding underprovides: MOSTLY AGREED" #2f6b8f x 500 y 652 font_size 38 hidden true
    text st2 "were they private? CONTESTED" #b5482e x 500 y 600 font_size 38 hidden true

    card title "Coase goes to the archives"

The textbooks were sure: a light can't be sold. In 1974 Rona (@draw coase@)ld Coase went to see how Britain's lighthouses had actually been paid for.
    draw claim

    erase coase

B: Between 1610 and 1675, Trinity House, the official lighthouse authority, built none. Private individuals built at least ten.
    draw n1610

And of the forty-six lighthouses standing in 1820, thirty-four had been built by private individuals.
    draw n1820 n1820b

    erase n1610 n1820 n1820b

B: So how did they get paid, if the beam can't be switched off?
    draw ship owner

At the port, not at sea. The owner got a paten (@draw crown e3 e3l@)t from the Crown, and ships paid a toll for each light they passed,  (@draw e2 e2l@)collected by customs when they docked.
    draw port e1 e1l

B: The beam can't keep anyone out, but the harbour can. Tie the light to something excludable, and it can be sold.
    highlight port effect box

Coase's verdict: economists should stop usi (@draw claim_x@)ng the lighthouse as a service only the government could provide.

B: But look at who (@highlight crown effect circle@) made that work. The Crown granted the monopoly and fixed the toll, its custo (@highlight port effect box@)ms officers collected it, and every ship paid, like it or not.
    draw e4 e4l

Critics such as Van Zandt and Bertrand call that a state-enforced tax, not a market. And in  (@draw buy@)1836 Parliament had Trinity House buy the private lights out.
    highlight e4 e4l

    erase buy claim claim_x

B: So how sure are we? That free riding leaves public goods u (@draw st1@)nderprovided is mostly agreed. Whether Coase's ligh (@draw st2@)ts were really private is contested.

And today, British lighthouses still run on light dues, paid by ships calling at British and Irish ports.
    text today "today: light dues at UK and Irish ports" cites ["trinity"] x 500 y 232 font_size 34

    quiz "A town wants a New Year's fireworks show, seen from every street, paid for by voluntary donations. What happens?" intro "Three new cases." voice b
        right: Less than it's worth: everyone sees the show whether they gave or not, so many don't give.
        wrong: Think of the skipper of boat three: if the others give, he sees the show anyway. Here, less than it's worth.
        + They raise less than the show is worth
        * They raise what it's worth
        * They raise more, since everyone enjoys it

    quiz "A TV channel broadcasts free over the air: anyone with an aerial can watch. How can a private firm pay for it?"
        right: Sell attention to advertisers: the signal can't keep anyone out, so charge someone who can be made to pay.
        wrong: If the viewers can't be made to pay, who can? Here, the advertisers.
        + Sell the viewers' attention to advertisers
        * Ask viewers for donations
        * Make the signal stronger

    quiz "A patent makes a new idea excludable. By Samuelson's argument, what is still lost?" voice b
        right: Uses that are priced out: one more user of an idea costs nothing, so any price above zero turns some away.
        wrong: One more user of an idea costs nothing, so any price turns someone away: here, the uses priced out.
        + Uses by people who value it but won't pay the price
        * Nothing: the market now works
        * The inventor's reward

So the market alone won't light the reef. Give it a harbour to charge in and a state to back the toll, and for two centuries it did.
    highlight port crown

    pause 0.5

